WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.502
-Hi, buddy, how are you?
-How are you?

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.670
-You know, you're now
our most frequent guest.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:04.970
-I was told
I pulled into the lead today.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.572
-Congratulations.
-Pretty --

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:08.341
I'm feeling pretty good
about it.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.942
-Big deal. Big day to do it.

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:13.046
-I just called my guy
who edits my Wikipedia page.

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:14.146
-You have a guy?
-No.

00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:15.982
-I got to get a guy to do that.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.084
-By the way,
you may just want to --

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:21.253
-You -- I think
leading up to today,

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:24.858
there were, rightfully so,
fears about safety,

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.927
fears about the many
COVID protocols

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.195
that would have to take place,

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:32.331
and that maybe clouded
what today could be.

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:34.834
And now that its happened
and we've seen the inauguration,

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.570
did you find optimism in it?

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.872
-A lot, yeah. I feel --

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.073
You know, the thing
I said last night was

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:43.643
my feeling has been like

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.213
if you've ever been
in a near-miss car accident,

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:52.251
that feeling afterwards of,
like, adrenaline and excitement.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:53.886
It's not joy. It's not like --

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:58.124
You're like, "Wow,
I came real close just there."

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:03.830
-Well, especially when you think
about the numerous negatives

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.733
this president had
in the minds of voters,

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.200
it was so close.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.836
-Right. Yes.
-Like it wasn't in the end,

00:01:10.836 --> 00:01:14.673
you know, again,
as a pure numerical vote count,

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:16.042
it was a repudiation,

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:18.811
but the electoral college
did not repudiate Donald Trump.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:20.012
-No, I mean, it was --

00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:23.782
Again, the whole wiring
of the system is faulty.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:25.518
I think that's clear, you know?

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.820
So, I think my --

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.490
I definitely felt real relief
and some joy today,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.992
some real hope

00:01:33.992 --> 00:01:38.131
about what we avoided
and how bad it could have been

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.933
and what we have,
through a lot of effort

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.535
by our fellow Americans

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.837
of all different kinds
of walks of life --

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:47.073
I mean, you know,
we have this thing, right,

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:49.742
where we think about
these, like,

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.444
you know,
there's the Rust Belt diners,

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.280
and then, there's the, like,
effete urban college grads

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.448
or whatever.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:56.882
It's a big country,
and there were people

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:58.517
who were going
to Black Lives Matter protests

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:00.052
in small towns in the Midwest,

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:03.255
and there were people who were
doing women's marches in Idaho.

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:05.024
And there's all kinds of people

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.259
from all kinds
of different backgrounds

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.228
who came together to say
this is not right.

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:10.229
And they did it for four years

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.498
and, in the end,
saved the country

00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:15.401
from what I think
would have been essentially

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.838
its last chapter as the
democracy that it used to be.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:21.841
-I am thinking, you know,
it's not quite a parallel,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.709
but you know, obviously,
one of the things

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:27.980
that President Biden
had to address today

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.150
is the fact that we have
this COVID crisis --

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.585
that, you know, I think

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:36.088
due to real exhaustion
with the numbers,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:38.524
with the attention
on both the election

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:40.360
and the aftermath
of the election --

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.896
And you have done a great job
of drawing attention to the fact

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:47.066
that we have just sort of
normalized this terrible crisis

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.467
with this growing death toll.

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:51.069
And much like -- You know,
the Obama/Biden administration

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:53.439
obviously came in in the middle
of the financial crisis,

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:56.576
but part of
when you think about the fact

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:59.212
that Donald Trump complained
about inauguration size,

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.347
like, there really
wasn't as much going on

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.216
insofar as, like,
a crisis to this magnitude.

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:06.319
-Yeah, I mean, again,
you keep searching

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:08.054
for, like, the right analog
or precedent,

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.222
and there is nothing great.

00:03:10.222 --> 00:03:12.291
I mean, you know,
it was striking to me --

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:16.396
You know, it is part
of our kind of civic sacraments

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.431
that we talk about Lincoln.

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:19.799
-Yeah.
-Because Lincoln, is, you know,

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:23.168
he is the sort of "sartemic"
figure, rightly so.

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:25.704
But today, it had -- Like,
when we talk about the union,

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.208
disunion, and uncivil war,
when you quote Lincoln,

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:31.945
you know,
Lincoln took over a nation

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:34.914
gripped in what would be its
probably worst crisis ever --

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.715
And FDR was close.

00:03:36.715 --> 00:03:39.184
And I remember
being on the steps in 2009

00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:41.621
when we were losing
800,000 jobs a month

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:44.189
and looked like the whole thing
could go under.

00:03:44.189 --> 00:03:47.259
This is even worse than that.
And I thought that --

00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:49.095
I think that Biden has done
a very good job,

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.529
I think the whole
incoming administration

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:53.299
has done a very good job
of centering COVID.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.568
I mean, one of
the weirdest things,

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.037
surreal and sadistic
in its own way,

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:00.139
was that
because of the narcissism

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.408
and sociopathic
kind of proclivities

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:06.612
of the last president, talking
about people dying from COVID

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:08.981
was viewed by him
and his faction

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:11.817
as an insult to him personally.

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:13.585
It was --
You couldn't talk about it,

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:18.190
because you were then
insulting Donald Trump.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.859
So, this bizarre silence
has gripped --

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:21.894
-Yeah.
-You know, I mean,

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.095
it's just not, you know,

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:25.264
a 41-year-old incoming
Republican congressman

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:28.634
from Louisiana died of COVID

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.503
before he could take office,

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:34.140
and it was just kind of
like a --

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.876
That's a horrific tragedy
for him, for his family,

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:39.679
for this bright life
the guy had ahead of him.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:41.080
But this --

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.951
I think this kind of heavy
blanket of swallowing silence

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:46.385
has been lifted,

00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:48.187
and that's a really
important thing.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:51.256
-It does strike me that --

00:04:51.256 --> 00:04:52.558
And, again, we'll see.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.159
And I think that,
you know, sometimes

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:55.595
we talk about tone a lot,

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:58.397
and right now, what we need
is, like, a plan and results.

00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:00.199
-Yeah.
-But it does strike me

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:04.603
that President Biden has
historically been pretty good

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:07.472
framing tragedy

00:05:07.472 --> 00:05:11.577
and both helping us understand
the way to approach it...

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:13.145
-Yeah.
-...and the way to process it.

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:16.448
And that really struck me
more last night than today.

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:19.285
But, you know, at the memorial
last night, you realized,

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:22.255
"Oh, this is a guy
who understands loss

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:25.725
and understands
moving forward from loss."

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:27.026
-Persistence and endurance.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:29.027
-Yeah.
-And mourning and keeping going.

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:32.264
Yeah, and I think, in that way,
there's a sort of --

00:05:32.264 --> 00:05:34.434
There is a kind of the cosmic
alignment of this guy

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:37.002
that, you know,
I joked today on Twitter

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.204
that the first time
he ran for president was 1988,

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:40.639
like "never let go
of your dreams."

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:42.308
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.176
It's an amazing fact,
this guy --

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.412
you know, 48 years
in public life.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.348
He's elected as the youngest
you could possibly be

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:51.850
to enter the Senate,
29 years old.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.587
Horrible tragedy.
Lives his entire career --

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:57.189
He's only done one thing
since he was 30...

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.225
-Yeah.
-...which is be a public figure

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.826
and --

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:05.230
And now, you know, at his age to
take over this country in crisis

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:06.398
having seen what he's seen

00:06:06.398 --> 00:06:07.800
and lived through
what he's lived through,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.702
like, there is a certain
kind of weightiness to it all.

00:06:10.702 --> 00:06:11.737
And I think you're right.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.038
I think his instincts
on it are right.

00:06:13.038 --> 00:06:15.274
Now, the other thing you said
is so true, also.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.743
Like, you know,

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:21.814
competence and getting stuff
done matters more than anything.

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.883
I mean, I thought
he hit the right notes today.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.952
There are some extremely,
supremely competent people

00:06:26.952 --> 00:06:30.089
that I think highly of
in that administration,

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:32.858
but it's not easy, the task
they have for themselves.

00:06:32.858 --> 00:06:33.959
It's not trivial.

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:35.794
-And it's really
important to note,

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:38.330
like, it was never going to be
important -- Oh, sorry --

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.399
It was never going to be easy
for any administration.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:41.934
-No.
-We were never saying

00:06:41.934 --> 00:06:43.668
it was easy
for the Trump administration

00:06:43.668 --> 00:06:45.103
to deal with this crisis,

00:06:45.103 --> 00:06:47.172
but, ultimately, at some point,
you have to have a plan.

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:48.974
I want to talk more with Chris.

00:06:48.974 --> 00:06:50.410
We'll be right back
right after this.

